'''Sant Cristòfor International Airport''' ({{lang|ca|Aeroport Internacional de Sant Cristòfor}}; [[IATA airport code|IATA]]: SCI, [[ICAO airport code|ICAO]]: SCIB) is the busiest airport in [[Balboa]] and the principal international gateway to the [[Isthmus of Balboa]]. Located in the [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|ciutat of Sant Martí]] in eastern [[Sant Cristòfor]], the airport serves the metropolitan area of approximately 5.16 million people and the nearby [[International City of Areza]]. The airport is a hub for the flag carrier [[Balboa Airlines]] and the low-cost carrier [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]].

The airport is notable for its dual-code arrangement: the '''Areza Terminal''', a separate facility serving flights where all passengers are ticketed to the [[International City of Areza]], operates under the secondary IATA code '''ARZ''' (ICAO: SCAR). This arrangement, unique in Adonia, was established as part of the [[Adonian Community]]'s consolidation of air services to Areza following the closure of [[Areza International Airport]] in 2003.

== History ==

=== Planning and construction ===

The need for a new airport to serve Sant Cristòfor was identified in the early 1970s, as the existing airport at [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]] — which had been rebuilt in 1954 during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate period]] — was constrained by its urban location and could not be expanded to accommodate jet-era traffic. The [[Balboa Airports Authority]] was established in 1975 to oversee the planning, construction, and operation of the new facility.

A site in [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]] was selected in 1977, on flat agricultural land approximately 12 km east of the city centre. The site offered room for expansion, clear approaches over the coastal plain, and proximity to the planned Metro Line 1 extension. Construction began in 1979 and was completed in 1984; the airport opened on 15 March 1985.

=== Two-terminal era (1985–2000) ===

The airport opened with two terminals: Terminal A (domestic) and Terminal B (international, serving Abyalan and Lurandian routes). The two terminals were connected by a central atrium with shared check-in, security, and baggage claim facilities. The initial capacity was approximately 12 million passengers per year.

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 1]] was extended to the airport in 1986, providing a direct rail link to the city centre via the [[Grand Train Terminal]]. The airport rapidly exceeded its design capacity, handling 18 million passengers by 1995, and congestion in Terminal B became a persistent issue.

=== Areza Terminal and the Areza consolidation (2000–2003) ===

[[File:Areza Terminal Sant Cristofor Airport placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|The Areza Terminal, the separate ARZ-coded terminal, with its own IATA code]]

The closure of [[Areza International Airport]] — the former [[Ciutat de l'Estació]] airport that had served the [[International City of Areza]] since 1954 — was mandated by the [[Adonian Community]] as part of the consolidation of Areza's transport infrastructure. The AC funded the construction of the Areza Terminal at Sant Cristòfor International Airport as a replacement facility, with the requirement that all flights serving Areza be consolidated at the new terminal.

The Areza Terminal opened in 2000 as a physically separate building approximately 800 metres south of the main terminal complex. It is connected to the main terminals by a covered walkway and a dedicated people mover. The terminal operates under the secondary IATA code '''ARZ''' (ICAO: SCAR), making Sant Cristòfor one of the few airports in Adonia with dual IATA codes. Any airline may operate ARZ-coded flights provided that all passengers on the flight are ticketed to Areza; passengers on ARZ-coded flights clear a dedicated Areza customs and immigration facility within the Areza Terminal rather than Balboan customs, and are transported to Areza via the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] express service.

The closure of Areza International Airport was completed in 2003, when [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] — running from Sant Martí through the canal corridor to Areza — was opened. The Line 9 extension was built with bypass tracks at all stations, allowing express services to skip intermediate stops and provide a direct, non-stop connection between the Areza Terminal and Areza in approximately 45 minutes.

=== Terminal C and modern expansion (2008–present) ===

Terminal C, a new international terminal serving flights to [[Kaftia]], [[Illypnia]], and [[Fosia]], opened in 2008 alongside a third runway (06/24), built to increase passenger capacity and to separate cargo operations from passenger traffic. Terminal C was built to relieve chronic congestion in Terminal B and to accommodate the growth of long-haul international traffic driven by Sant Cristòfor's expanding role as a financial hub. Terminal C is the largest of the four terminals and is connected to the main complex by an extension of the central atrium.

== Infrastructure ==

=== Terminals ===

[[File:Sant Cristofor Airport terminal layout placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|Terminal layout of Sant Cristòfor International Airport]]

The airport has four terminals:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Terminal !! Opened !! Function !! Gates !! Annual capacity
|-
| A || 1985 || Domestic || 18 || ~10 million
|-
| B || 1985 || International (Abyala / Lurandia) || 22 || ~12 million
|-
| C || 2008 || International (Kaftia / Illypnia / Fosia) || 28 || ~15 million
|-
| Areza || 2000 || Areza services (ARZ code) || 8 || ~5 million
|-
| '''Total''' || || '''76''' || '''~42 million'''
|}

Terminals A, B, and C are connected by a central atrium with shared check-in, security screening, and baggage claim facilities on the landside, and post-security airside corridors linking the gate areas. The '''Areza Terminal''' is a separate building approximately 800 metres south of the main complex, connected by a covered walkway and a people mover. The Areza Terminal has its own dedicated check-in, security, customs, and baggage claim facilities; passengers on ARZ-coded flights clear [[International City of Areza|Areza]] customs and immigration rather than Balboan, and do not enter Balboan territory. This distinction is significant because Areza maintains a 15-day open visa policy for all [[Adonian Community]] member states, whereas Balboa applies its own separate visa requirements. Passengers travelling to Areza who would require a Balboan visa for transit through the main terminals can therefore use ARZ-coded flights without needing to meet Balboan immigration requirements.

The dual-code arrangement shapes airline routing decisions: countries with favourable visa relationships with Balboa generally route their passengers through the main SCI terminals, while those whose nationals would face Balboan visa restrictions route through the ARZ-coded Areza Terminal to access Areza's more open visa regime.

A '''transfer desk''' in the international terminals (B and C) allows passengers arriving on SCI-coded flights who wish to travel to Areza to transfer airside to the Areza Terminal arrivals area, where they clear Areza customs and immigration rather than Balboan. This enables passengers on any international flight to reach Areza without passing through Balboan border control, even if their flight was not ARZ-coded.

Unlike the main terminals, the Areza Terminal has no public arrivals or departures area for private vehicles. Access to the Areza Terminal is restricted to authorised transport only: the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] express service, scheduled bus services, chartered car services, and [[Adonian Community]] government vehicles. Private vehicles are not permitted at the Areza Terminal. Authorised ground vehicles travelling between the Areza Terminal and Areza are restricted to the [[E54 expressway]], a dedicated road that runs directly between the airport and Areza without intermediate junctions or access to the Balboan road network; vehicles using the E54 are not permitted to stop in Balboa and may only travel between the Areza Terminal and Areza.

=== Runways ===

The airport has three runways:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Direction !! Length !! Surface !! Use
|-
| 09/27 || 3,200 m || Asphalt || Primary; all commercial passenger operations
|-
| 15/33 || 2,600 m || Asphalt || Secondary; domestic, general aviation, relief
|-
| 06/24 || 2,800 m || Asphalt || Cargo; also passenger overflow during peak
|}

Runway 09/27 is the primary runway, handling the majority of commercial passenger operations. Runway 15/33 is used for domestic services, general aviation, and as a relief runway during peak periods. Runway 06/24, opened in 2008, was built primarily to separate cargo operations from passenger traffic, but also serves as passenger overflow during peak periods. All three runways are equipped with [[Instrument Landing System|ILS]] Category II approach systems.

=== Cargo facilities ===

Sant Cristòfor International Airport is a major cargo hub, reflecting the city's position at the junction of the Illypnian, Fosian, and Kaftian markets and its proximity to the [[Balboa Canal]]. The airport handled 612,000 tonnes of cargo in 2025, making it the busiest cargo airport in the isthmus region. Cargo facilities are located on the eastern side of the airport, adjacent to Runway 15/33, with dedicated cargo aprons, warehousing, and logistics facilities operated by the [[Balboa Airports Authority]] and private logistics companies.

== Airlines and destinations ==

=== Hub operations ===

[[Balboa Airlines]], the national flag carrier, maintains its primary hub at the airport, operating domestic and international routes from Terminals A, B, and C. [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]], a low-cost carrier, operates domestic and short-haul international routes primarily from Terminal A.

=== ARZ-coded services ===

The ARZ code is used for flights where all passengers are ticketed to the [[International City of Areza]]. Any airline may operate ARZ-coded flights from the Areza Terminal, provided that all passengers on the flight hold confirmed onward travel to Areza. In practice, ARZ-coded flights are operated primarily by the flag carriers of [[Adonian Community]] member states, reflecting the AC's role as the principal destination for diplomatic, institutional, and official travel to Areza. Passengers on ARZ-coded flights clear Areza customs and immigration within the Areza Terminal and are transported to Areza via the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] express service, which operates non-stop between the Areza Terminal station and Areza station in approximately 45 minutes. The ARZ arrangement eliminates the need for passengers travelling to Areza to clear Balboan customs or meet Balboan visa requirements, streamlining the connection between international flights and the AC institutions. This is of particular importance because Areza maintains a 15-day open visa policy for all [[Adonian Community]] member states, whereas Balboa applies its own separate visa requirements — meaning that nationals of AC member states who would require a Balboan visa for transit through the main terminals can travel to Areza via ARZ-coded flights without restriction.

== Ground transport ==

=== Metro ===

{{main|Metro de Sant Cristòfor}}

The airport is served by two lines of the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor]], with two separate station entrances:

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Line 1]] connects the airport to the city centre via the [[Grand Train Terminal]]. The airport extension operates an express service from the airport to the edge of [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], skipping intermediate stops, before running local for the final 7 km through Original's dense urban core. The journey time from the airport to the Grand Train Terminal is approximately 20 minutes. All-stations services also operate on the same tracks at a lower frequency.

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Line 9]] connects the airport to the [[International City of Areza|International City of Areza]] via the canal corridor, a distance of 51.2 km, with a journey time of approximately 45 minutes on the express service. Line 9 was built with bypass tracks at all intermediate stations, allowing express services to skip local stops and provide a direct connection between the Areza Terminal and Areza. The express service operates every 10 minutes during peak hours and every 15 minutes off-peak; all-stations services operate on the same tracks at a lower frequency.

The airport has two Metro entrances. The '''Airport Transit Centre''' station, located beneath the central atrium, provides direct access to Terminals A, B, and C and is served by both Line 1 and Line 9 (local and express). The '''Areza Terminal''' station, located adjacent to the Areza Terminal, is served only by Line 9 express services, providing a direct connection to Areza without passing through the main terminal complex.

=== Road ===

The main terminals (A, B, and C) are connected to the city road network via the '''E51''', which runs east from [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] to the airport. The E51 connects to the [[Eastern Highway, Sant Cristòfor|Eastern Highway]] (E52), the elevated expressway running through Original, providing onward access to the city centre and the wider road network. Journey time by road to Original is approximately 25–40 minutes depending on traffic. Taxi, ride-hailing, and private vehicle access is available at the main terminals. Long-term and short-term parking is provided in multi-storey car parks adjacent to each terminal.

The '''[[E54 expressway]]''' is a dedicated road connecting the Areza Terminal directly to the [[International City of Areza]]. The E54 has no intermediate junctions and no access to the Balboan road network; vehicles using it may only travel between the Areza Terminal and Areza, and are not permitted to stop in Balboa. Access to the E54 is restricted to authorised vehicles: [[Adonian Community]] government vehicles, chartered car services, and scheduled bus services. Private vehicles are not permitted on the E54 or at the Areza Terminal.

=== Bus ===

The [[Autoritat de Transport de Sant Cristòfor]] (ATS) operates airport bus routes connecting the airport to major hotels, the cruise terminal, and the ciutats not served by the Metro. Airport bus services depart from the ground transportation centre beneath the central atrium.

== Accidents and incidents ==

* '''14 February 1988''' — [[Balboa Airlines]] Flight 107, a [[Boeing 737-200|Boeing 737]] arriving from [[Sant Bart]] with 112 passengers and 6 crew, overran Runway 09/27 on landing during a heavy rainstorm. The aircraft came to rest 140 metres beyond the runway threshold, its nose gear collapsing on the soft ground. All 118 occupants evacuated safely via emergency slides; 9 passengers sustained minor injuries. The investigation attributed the overrun to aquaplaning on the rain-soaked runway and a tailwind component that exceeded the airline's landing limits. Following the incident, the Balboa Airports Authority installed grooved surface treatment on both runways and revised wet-weather landing minima.

* '''3 September 1993''' — A runway incursion occurred when a [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]] Boeing 737 entered Runway 09/27 without clearance while a [[Balboa Airlines]] Airbus A310 was on its take-off roll. The A310's crew aborted take-off at approximately 120 knots, stopping approximately 400 metres short of the intersecting RapidAir aircraft. The investigation found that the RapidAir crew had misinterpreted a taxi clearance as a line-up clearance, and that the air traffic controller's phraseology had been ambiguous. The incident prompted the Balboa Airports Authority to implement mandatory readback procedures for all runway entry clearances and to install enhanced taxiway lighting.

* '''22 November 2001''' — [[Volisanian Airlines]] Flight 614, an [[Airbus A300|Airbus A300-600]] arriving from [[Volisania]] with 187 passengers and 12 crew, suffered a collapse of the left main landing gear on touchdown on Runway 09/27. The aircraft veered left onto the grass shoulder, coming to rest at an angle to the runway. All 199 occupants evacuated via emergency slides; 22 passengers and 2 crew members sustained injuries, none life-threatening. The investigation determined that the landing gear had suffered a fatigue fracture in the side strut, a known issue on the A300-600 that had been the subject of an airworthiness directive. The aircraft was written off. The incident accelerated the retirement of the A300-600 from the Volisanian Airlines fleet.

* '''7 June 2006''' — A bird strike on a [[Balboa Airlines]] Boeing 767 departing for [[Portnou]] caused both engines to surge during the initial climb. The crew returned the aircraft to the airport and landed safely on Runway 09/27. The subsequent inspection found significant damage to both engine fan blades from a flock of large seabirds. The incident led to the implementation of a wildlife hazard management programme at the airport, including habitat modification around the airfield and radar-assisted bird detection systems.

== Relationship with other airports ==

{{main|Sant Cristòfor Els Aiguamolls Airport}}

[[Sant Cristòfor Els Aiguamolls Airport]], located in the neighbouring [[Els Aiguamolls]] comarca just north of the city boundary, serves as a complementary facility rather than a relief airport. Els Aiguamolls handles domestic and short-haul regional routes, and is used primarily by residents of the northern ciutats — particularly [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]] — seeking connections to [[Sant Bart]] and [[Nurra]] without transiting the busier Sant Cristòfor International. [[Balboa Airlines]] operates services from Els Aiguamolls, and the airport is connected to the city by commuter rail. The two airports are not formally coordinated but serve distinct markets: Sant Cristòfor International for long-haul and high-density domestic operations, and Els Aiguamolls for domestic point-to-point and regional services.

